In response to James's report feedback regarding the distinction between average economic impacts (like GDP in 2050) and vulnerability to shocks, I proposed integrating this insight into the section on climate risks and adaptation strategies. I suggested that he add a clarification after discussing reactive and anticipatory adaptation actions, emphasizing that small island states face significant challenges from extreme events despite manageable long-term economic projections. The addition underscores the importance of integrating stress testing for extreme shocks into economic assessments, enhancing the discourse around adaptation investments and strategies while addressing the nuances highlighted in the feedback.
